      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "Spinal cord stimulation reduces pain of critical ischaemia in patients with severe inoperable coronary artery and peripheral vascular disease by increasing microvascular flow. Patients with cardiac pacemaker may be denied a spinal cord stimulator (SCS) implant because of the risk of compromising pacemaker function by inhibition or reversion to asynchronous noise-pacing mode. We describe the management of a patient with an SCS implant for lower limb ischaemia who required a pacemaker. We suggest that with modern pacemakers it is safe to implant a spinal cord stimulator simultaneously with a pacemaker provided adequate precautions are taken to prevent interdevice interference."},
